window.docContents[30].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-the-edge-count-formula":{"contents":"A fundamental fact about finite trees: a tree on n vertices has exactly n - 1\nedges. Mathlib states this as #G.edgeFinset + 1 = Fintype.card V, which avoids the\ntruncated subtraction of ℕ: working with n - 1 would need the side condition\n1 ≤ n at every step.\n\n#check IsTree.card_edgeFinset -- #G.edgeFinset + 1 = Fintype.card V\n\n\nConversely, a finite connected graph with this many edges is a tree.\n\n#check isTree_iff_connected_and_card\n\n\nMore generally, a connected graph satisfies |V| ≤ |E(G)| + 1. This is stated with\nNat.card, so it needs no finiteness assumption, but for an infinite graph it says\nnothing: Nat.card of an infinite type is the junk value 0, so for pathNat₁ it\nsays 0 ≤ 0 + 1.\n\n#check Connected.card_vert_le_card_edgeSet_add_one\n\n\nAny nontrivial finite tree has at least two leaves (vertices of degree one), and so\nits minimum degree is 1. (Note Nontrivial V is the typeclass saying that V\ncontains at least two elements.)\n\n#check IsTree.exists_ne_and_degree_eq_one\n#check IsTree.minDegree_eq_one_of_nontrivial\n\n\nDeleting a leaf from a connected graph leaves it connected. This is the basic tool\nfor proving results about finite trees by induction on the number of vertices.\n\n#check Connected.induce_compl_singleton_of_degree_eq_one\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Trees","header":"10.2.3. The Edge Count Formula","id":"/Connectivity/#section-the-edge-count-formula"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-deciding-adjacency":{"contents":"Where does an instance of DecidableRel G.Adj used by #eval and decide come\nfrom? Usually we would like Lean to synthesize these automatically by typeclass\ninference.\n\nTo see which instance is being synthesized for a particular graph we can use the\n#synth command:\n\n#synth DecidableRel K[5].Adj -- Top.adjDecidable (Fin 5) a b\n\n\n#check SimpleGraph.Top.adjDecidable -- the instance requires [DecidableEq V]\n\n\nSo the instance of decidable adjacency in the complete graph requires an instance of\nDecidableEq, for its vertex type. This is natural: any two vertices in a complete\ngraph are adjacent if and only if they are distinct. So deciding whether or not two\nvertices are adjacent in a complete graph is the same as deciding whether or not they\nare equal.\n\nNote that for the empty graph (on any type) we always have decidability of the\nadjacency relation, since no vertices are adjacent. This holds even on ℝ, where\nequality itself is undecidable.\n\n#synth DecidableRel (⊥ : SimpleGraph ℝ).Adj\n\n\n#check SimpleGraph.Bot.adjDecidable -- no assumption on V is required\n\n\nIf we revisit the three definitions of the path graph on ℕ from\nSimpleGraph we can ask two obvious questions: do they\nhave decidable adjacency and if so will Lean automatically synthesize the required\ninstance in each case?\n\nThe answer to the first question should be independent of which definition we use: as\nwe already proved they all define the same graph! So what would a method for deciding\nadjacency in this graph look like? Given a : ℕ and b : ℕ we need to check if\na + 1 = b or b + 1 = a holds. Since ℕ has decidable equality this is clearly\ndecidable.\n\nTurning to the second question of whether or not Lean will automatically synthesize\nthe required instance we see that the exact form of our definition matters. It turns\nout that Lean can only infer the required instance for pathNat₁.\n\n#source pathNat₁ -- abbrev pathNat₁ : SimpleGraph ℕ where\n                 --   Adj x y := x + 1 = y ∨ y + 1 = x\n\n\n#synth DecidableRel pathNat₁.Adj\n          -- fun a b ↦ instDecidableOr\n          -- @instDecidableOr (a + 1 = b) (b + 1 = a) (instDecidableEqNat (a + 1) b)\n          -- (instDecidableEqNat (b + 1) a) : Decidable (a + 1 = b ∨ b + 1 = a)\n\n\nSince pathNat₁ is an abbrev Lean can see through its definition to find\npathNat₁.Adj a b := a + 1 = b ∨ b + 1 = a. Since Or is decidable and each of the\npropositions a + 1 = b and b + 1 = a are decidable (using decidable equality on\nℕ) Lean can infer decidability of adjacency in pathNat₁.\n\nFor pathNat₂ our definition is a def rather than an abbrev.\n\n#source pathNat₂ -- def pathNat₂ : SimpleGraph ℕ := SimpleGraph.fromRel (· + 1 = ·)\n\n\nLean can't see through the def so fails to infer the required instance.\n\n/- error: failed to synthesize\n DecidableRel pathNat₂.Adj\n-/\n#synth DecidableRel pathNat₂.Adj -- fails\n\n\nIf we change the def to an abbrev then it works!\n\nabbrev pathNat₂' : SimpleGraph ℕ := SimpleGraph.fromRel (· + 1 = ·)\n#synth DecidableRel pathNat₂'.Adj --fun a b ↦ instDecidableRelAdjFromRelOfDecidableEq\n                                  --             (fun x1 x2 ↦ x1 + 1 = x2) a b\n\n\nLean sees through the abbrev and finds\npathNat₂' := SimpleGraph.fromRel (· + 1 = ·). It then finds the instance\ninstDecidableRelAdjFromRelOfDecidableEq which is the instance of decidable\nadjacency for a graph defined from a decidable relation using the\nSimpleGraph.fromRel constructor. In our case the relation is (· + 1 = ·), i.e.\nfun x y => x + 1 = y whose decidability can be inferred from the decidable equality\nof ℕ.\n\nRather than changing the definition of pathNat₂ to an abbrev we can also provide\na decidability instance for pathNat₂.Adj directly in two different ways.\nOne way is by unfolding its definition and then calling infer_instance. The\nsecond way is to use inferInstanceAs to specify the type of the instance we want to\nfind. In the latter case we know that Mathlib contains an instance of DecidableRel\nfor the adjacency relation of a graph defined using SimpleGraph.fromRel so we tell\nit to use this.\n\ninstance myDecidableRelPathNat₂ : DecidableRel pathNat₂.Adj := by\n  unfold pathNat₂\n  -- goal is now ⊢ DecidableRel (fromRel fun x1 x2 ↦ x1 + 1 = x2).Adj\n  -- so Lean can use the instance instDecidableRelAdjFromRelOfDecidableEq\n  infer_instance\n\n\nexample : DecidableRel pathNat₂.Adj :=\n  inferInstanceAs (DecidableRel (SimpleGraph.fromRel _).Adj)\n\n\nFinally we have pathNat₃ which was defined using SimpleGraph.fromEdgeSet. Lean\nagain fails to infer the required instance (despite this being an abbrev).\n\n#source pathNat₃ -- abbrev pathNat₃ : SimpleGraph ℕ := SimpleGraph.fromEdgeSet\n                 --                                       {e | ∃ n, e = s(n, n + 1)}\n\n\n/- error: failed to synthesize\n DecidableRel pathNat₃.Adj\n-/\n#synth DecidableRel pathNat₃.Adj\n\n\nThe problem here is that although Mathlib contains an instance for the decidability\nof adjacency in any graph built by the SimpleGraph.fromEdgeSet constructor, this\ninstance requires that membership of the defining edge set be decidable. But Lean\ndoesn't know how to decide whether or not a given edge s(a, b) is in the edge set\n{e | ∃ n, e = s(n, n + 1)}.\n\n#check instDecidableRelAdjFromEdgeSetOfDecidablePredSym2MemSetOfDecidableEq\n          -- {V : Type u} → (s : Set (Sym2 V)) → [DecidablePred fun x ↦ x ∈ s]\n          --      → [DecidableEq V] → DecidableRel (fromEdgeSet s).Adj\n\n\nOne solution in this case is to use the fact, which we already proved, that\npathNat₃ is equal to pathNat₁ and that Lean can infer decidable adjacency of\npathNat₁.\n\nHere we transfer the decidability instance from pathNat₁ to pathNat₃ using\ndecidable_of_iff. Defining it by rewriting with rw [pathNat₃_eq_pathNat₁]\nwould also type-check and work with #eval, but it would leave decide permanently\nstuck: see Decidability And Kernel Reduction for an explanation.\n\ninstance : DecidableRel (pathNat₃.Adj) := fun u v =>\n  decidable_of_iff (pathNat₁.Adj u v) (by rw [pathNat₃_eq_pathNat₁])\n\n\nexample : pathNat₃.Adj 42 43 := by decide\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Decidability","header":"4.1.4. Deciding Adjacency","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-deciding-adjacency"}});